   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to establishing a temporary\npractice authorization pilot program for certain medical and dental\nprofessionals to practice in underserved areas; and providing for the repeal\nof such provisions upon expiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S8341",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8341-2025",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 60,
       "fullName": "Patrick M. Gallivan",
       "imgName": "427_patrick_m._gallivan.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 427,
       "sessionMemberId": 2377,
       "sessionYear": 2025,
       "shortName": "GALLIVAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§6907 & 6525, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8341\n\nSPONSOR: STAVISKY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to establishing a tempo-\nrary practice authorization pilot program for certain medical and dental\nprofessionals to practice in underserved areas; and providing for the\nrepeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo grant out-of-state licensed medical and dental professionals who have\napplied for licensure in New York temporary authorization, for up to six\nmonths, to practice in counties or facilities designated as underserved,\npending full New York licensure\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Education Law § 6907 to grant temporary authorization\nto out-of-state registered professional nurses, licensed practical nurs-\nes, and nurse practitioners to practice in a county or facility desig-\nnated by the federal government as medically underserved, pending full\nlicensure.\n\nSection 2 amends Education Law § 6958 to grant temporary authorization\nto out-of-state licensed midwives to practice in a county or facility\ndesignated by the federal government as medically underserved, pending\nfull licensure.\n\nSection 3 amends Education Law § 6525 to grant temporary authorization\nto out-of-state board-certified physicians to practice in a county or\nfacility designated by the federal government as medically underserved,\npending full licensure.\n\nSection 4 amends Education Law § 6546 to grant temporary authorization\nto out-of-state licensed physician assistants to practice in a county or\nfacility designated by the federal government as medically underserved,\npending full licensure.\n\nSection 5 amends Education Law § 6605 to grant temporary authorization\nto out-of-state licensed dentists to practice in a county or facility\ndesignated by the federal government as underserved in the discipline of\ndental health, pending full licensure.\n\nSection 6 requires the education commissioner to provide a report evalu-\nating the pilot program.\n\nSection 7 establishes the effective and repeal dates.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThis bill increases healthcare staffing resources, including registered\nnurses, licensed practical nurses and nurse practitioners, in designated\nshortage areas by creating a pilot program to permit certain out-of-\nstate practitioners to receive a 180-day temporary license upon their\napplication for a permanent New York license. The temporary license will\nbe issued to out-of-state nurses, nurse-midwives, physician assistants,\nphysicians and dentists so they may immediately begin working in hospi-\ntals, nursing homes and other facilities in New York's underserved\nareas. The bill does not apply to \"travel\" nurses or any other health\ncare professional employed by an employment agency. Instead, to qualify\nfor a temporary license, the employer must be a health care facility.\n\nAccording to the federal government, New York state has primary care\nhealth professional shortages in 119 geographic areas and facilities,\nand dental health professional shortages in 117 facilities.' These shor-\ntages occur throughout the state, in rural and urban areas. New York\nparticularly needs more nurses in these areas, but New York facilities\noften have difficulty recruiting out-of-state practitioners because New\nYork, unlike other states in the country, does not permit out-of-state\npractitioners to practice in the interim period during which the Depart-\nment of Education (SED) accepts and reviews an application for permanent\nlicensure.\n\nTo determine the pilot program's effectiveness, this bill directs SED to\nprovide an annual written evaluation examining its success at increasing\nthe healthcare workforce in identified shortage areas.  Similar work-\nforce studies have already been successfully undertaken in the past by\nthe Center for Health Workforce Studies at the School of Public Health,\nState University of New York (SUNY) at Albany.2\n\nIt is important to note that this bill does not create a \"compact\", as\nit does not permit out-of-state practitioners to work in New York under\nthe authority of their out-of-state license. Rather, the pilot program\naims to improve staffing in healthcare facilities in shortage areas by\nenabling the facilities to hire and employ licensed out-of-state medical\nand dental practitioners who have received, and will practice under, a\ntemporary New York State license issued by SED. Authority to practice\nunder the temporary license ends after no more than 180 days. By\nincreasing staff resources in medically underserved areas, facilities\nwill improve access to patient care.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinimal.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire and be deemed\nrepealed 3 years after it shall have become a law.\n1 Bureau of Health Workforce, Health Resources and Services Adminis-\ntration, U.S. Department of Health & Human Services. Designated Health\nProfessional Shortage Areas Statistics, Second Quarter of Fiscal Year\n2025 Designated HPSA Quarterly Summary.  As of March 31, 2025.\n2 Center for Health Workforce Studies, School of Public Health, Univer-\nsity at Albany, State University of New York. The Health Care Workforce\nin New York State: Trends in the Supply of and Demand for Health Care\nWorkers, April 2023.\n",
